# Component & Code Organization

Keep components focused on rendering and local UI state. Avoid bloated files.

## When to extract logic

Move logic out of a component when it:

- Has multiple responsibilities (fetching, transforming, formatting, and rendering)
- Exceeds ~150 lines or is hard to scan at a glance
- Contains reusable pure functions (formatters, validators, mappers)
- Talks to APIs or external systems

## Where to put extracted code

| Type | Location | Example |
|------|----------|---------|
| Pure helpers | `src/utils/` | `formatDate.ts`, `cn.ts` |
| API / async work | `src/services/` | `cvService.ts`, `jobScraperService.ts` |
| Reusable stateful logic | `src/hooks/` | `useJobList.ts` |
| Shared UI | `src/components/` | `Button.tsx`, `Sidebar.tsx` |
| Route views | `src/pages/` | `GenerateCV.tsx` |

## Code splitting

- Use React Router lazy loading for pages when the app grows:

```tsx
const GenerateCV = lazy(() => import('./pages/GenerateCV'))
```

- Prefer named exports in utils/services for tree-shaking
- Do not import heavy service modules into lightweight UI components unless needed

## Component guidelines

- One primary concern per component
- Co-locate small, component-specific helpers only if they are not reused elsewhere
- Pages compose components; they should not contain deep business logic

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — page stays thin
export default function GenerateCV() {
  const { data, isLoading } = useCvGenerator()
  return <CvForm data={data} isLoading={isLoading} />
}

// ❌ AVOID — API calls, transforms, and JSX all in one file
export default function GenerateCV() {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null)
  useEffect(() => { fetch('/api/cv').then(...) }, [])
  // 200 lines of logic + markup...
}
```
